course: technology
lesson: 15
title: Kitchen and garden dangers
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The kitchen and the garden are full of machines that cut, spin or get very hot, which is exactly why they are so useful, and exactly why they can hurt.
explain: A blade that chops carrots in a second will chop a finger just as fast; a pan that boils pasta will scald skin just as well.
explain: Most accidents happen in an ordinary moment: a toddler pulls a dangling cable, a cup of tea is left at the edge of the table, a stone flies out from under the mower.
explain: So these machines come with simple rules, and the most important word in them is «never»: never hands in, never alone, never close by while it runs.
explain: Knowing why the rules exist makes you the one who spots the danger and tells a grown-up.
fact: A blender spins sharp blades thousands of times a minute; it only works with its lid locked on, and an adult unplugs it before cleaning.
fact: A food processor or mincer has a narrow feed tube on purpose, so a hand cannot fit; food is pushed in with the pusher, never with fingers.
fact: A sharp knife cuts by putting all your push onto a tiny edge; a blunt knife is more dangerous because it slips, and nobody tries to catch a falling knife.
fact: Children use knives only with an adult, cutting on a board and away from themselves.
fact: Hobs and pans stay hot long after they are switched off, so pan handles are turned inward and nobody reaches over a hob.
fact: Many burns to small children happen in the kitchen, from pulled-down pans and cups of hot drink.
fact: A hot drink can still scald a small child about a quarter of an hour after it was made, so it is kept away from table edges and never passed over a baby.
fact: An iron stays hot long after it is switched off, and a dangling cable can be pulled so that the iron falls.
fact: A lawnmower blade can spin about fifty times a second and can throw stones, so children stay indoors or far away while it runs and never ride on or near a ride-on mower.
fact: A strimmer's nylon string spins so fast it cuts like a blade and throws stones and bits, which is why gardeners wear goggles and everyone else stays well away.
fact: Bleach and other cleaners are strong chemicals; they are never drunk, sniffed or mixed, because mixing some cleaners makes a poisonous gas.
fact: Some cleaners have a bitter taste added to stop people swallowing them, and they are kept locked away from small children.
fact: A shredder's blades can catch fingers, hair, ties and sleeves; many have a slot too thin for a small child's fingers, and children do not use one alone.
exercise: Why does a blender only work with its lid locked on? | So nothing can reach the spinning blades | So the food stays warm | So the motor goes faster | So the jug doesn't get dusty
exercise: Why is the feed tube of a food processor so narrow? | So a hand cannot fit inside | So the food is chopped up much faster | So less food spills out over the top | So it is easier to wash and dry later
exercise: What do you use to push food into a food processor? | The pusher, never fingers | Two fingers, held together carefully | A metal spoon | Your whole hand, quickly
exercise: Why is a blunt knife more dangerous than a sharp one? | It slips more easily | It is heavier to hold | It is made of weaker metal | It cuts deeper by itself
exercise: A knife slides off the counter. What do you do? | Step back and let it fall | Catch it quickly by the wooden handle | Catch it with both hands before it lands | Kick it away hard as it falls down
exercise: Why are pan handles turned inward on the hob? | So nobody knocks or pulls a pan over | So the handles stay cold | So the pan cooks faster | So the lid fits better
exercise: Why should you never hold a baby and a hot drink together? | The drink can spill and scald the baby | Babies dislike the smell of tea and coffee | The drink cools down too fast | The cup is too heavy for you
exercise: How long can a cup of tea stay hot enough to scald a small child? | About a quarter of an hour | Only about ten seconds | Only while it is steaming a lot | Not at all once milk is added
exercise: Why does the cable of an iron on an ironing board matter so much? | A dangling cable can be pulled so the hot iron falls | The cable gets hotter than the iron | The cable makes the iron heavier | The cable keeps the steam inside
exercise: Why must you keep away even from the side of a running mower? | The blade can throw stones out | The grass might fly into your hair | The mower might roll towards the sun | The noise might break windows
exercise: About how many times a second can a mower blade spin? | About fifty | About once | About five | About a million
exercise: Why do gardeners wear goggles with a strimmer? | It throws stones and bits of plant | The noise hurts their eyes | The sun is very bright in most gardens in summer | The string glows very bright
exercise: Why must cleaning products never be mixed? | Some mixtures make a poisonous gas | They lose their nice fresh smell and stop working | They turn into water | They become too thick to pour
exercise: Why do some cleaners taste bitter on purpose? | To stop people swallowing them | To make them clean better | To scare away flies | To keep them fresh for longer on the shelf
exercise: Why is a shredder slot so thin? | So small fingers cannot fit in | So it shreds the paper much faster | So the paper stays dry inside the bin | So it saves electricity every time
exercise: Grandad's mowing the lawn. Where should you be? | Indoors or far away | Right behind him, helping | Sitting on the mower | Picking up stones in front of it
exercise: 3 kids each want 2 slices of apple cut. How many slices does the adult cut? | 6 | 5 | 8 | 3
test: my sister says pass the tea over the baby, she's asleep anyway. ok? | No, never pass a hot drink over a baby | Yes, sleeping babies can't really get burned | Yes, if the cup is half full | No, but over a toddler is fine
test: oi, blender's jammed, stick a spoon in and give it a stir. What's right? | No: never put anything in; an adult unplugs it first | Yes, a wooden spoon is fine | Yes, if you're quick | Just put your fingers in instead
test: What would happen if a toddler pulled the cable of an iron left on the board? | The hot iron could fall and burn them | Nothing, irons have no weight | The iron would switch itself on and start steaming | The board would fold up safely
test: Ben reckons a sharp knife is always more dangerous than a blunt one. Right? | No, a blunt knife slips and can cut you more easily | Yes, sharp knives jump by themselves | Yes, blunt knives never cut skin | No, blunt knives can't cut anything
test: In a cartoon a dog rides on a ride-on mower with the farmer. What's the real rule? | Never ride on or near a ride-on mower | It's fine as long as you hold on really tight | It's fine for small children | It's fine on flat grass
test: Mum's under the sink and says «don't mix those two bottles». Why not? | Some cleaners make a poisonous gas when mixed | They will stop cleaning | It wastes the bottles | They turn green and sticky and ruin the cupboard
test: The hob's been off for 5 minutes. Is it safe to touch? | Not yet; hobs stay hot after they are off | Yes, hobs cool down in a few seconds once switched off | Yes, if the light is off | No, because it is still cooking
test: shut up and help me feed this shredder, use both hands. What's the rule? | Keep fingers, hair and sleeves away, and don't use it alone | Push the paper in with your fingers | Wear a tie so you look smart | Use a ruler to push it deeper
test: Which is true about a hot drink on the table? | It should be kept away from the edge where little ones can reach | It is safe once you stop seeing steam | Babies can't pull tablecloths | Adding milk makes it unable to burn
test: Your little cousin finds bleach under the sink. What do you do? | Take it away from them and tell an adult | Let them sniff it once so they learn the smell | Pour it all down the sink so it is gone for good | Give it a quick taste to check what it is
test: Why does everyone stand far away when the strimmer is going? | It throws stones and bits that can hit you | It is too loud to be near without ear muffs on | It might run out of string | It blows cold air
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the feed tube of a food processor is made narrow. | To prevent the insertion of a hand | To increase the speed of processing | To reduce the wastage of food | To facilitate the cleaning of the device
exam: It is given that a cup of tea was prepared fifteen minutes previously. Ascertain whether it may still scald a young child. | It may still be hot enough to scald | It is certainly incapable of scalding | It may scald only an adult | It may scald only if milk is absent
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the hazard of a lawnmower to persons standing nearby? | Its blade may eject stones at considerable speed | It emits a gas which renders bystanders unconscious | Its noise may fracture nearby windows | It attracts insects which may sting
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the mixing of household cleaning products. | Certain combinations liberate a poisonous gas | All combinations improve cleaning power | Mixing renders the products harmless | Mixing is required before use
